===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{senseid|en|price paid}} {{lb|en|uncountable|finance}} The price paid for obtaining, or price received for providing, [[money]] or [[goods]] in a [[credit]] [[transaction]], calculated as a fraction of the amount or value of what was [[borrow]]ed. {{defdate|from earlier 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Our bank offers borrowers an annual '''interest''' of 5%.}}
# {{senseid|en|reward earned}} {{lb|en|uncountable|finance}} Any excess over and above an exact equivalent
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|IV|3|text=You shall have your desires with '''interest'''}}
# {{senseid|en|heightened attention as curiosity}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} A great [[attention]] and concern from someone or something; intellectual [[curiosity]]. {{defdate|from later 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|He has a lot of '''interest''' in vintage cars.}}
#: {{ux|en|I have a lot of '''interest''' in doing online coding.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=10|passage=The skipper Mr. Cooke had hired at Far Harbor was a God-fearing man with a luke warm '''interest''' in his new billet and employer, and had only been prevailed upon to take charge of the yacht after the offer of an emolument equal to half a year's sea pay of an ensign in the navy.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=Thinks I to myself, &ldquo;Sol, you're run off your course again. This is a rich man's summer &lsquo;cottage&rsquo; and if you don't look out there's likely to be some nice, lively dog taking an '''interest''' in your underpinning.&rdquo;}}
# {{senseid|en|heightened attention most broadly}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} Attention that is given to or received from someone or something.
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage={{...}} St. Bede's at this period of its history was perhaps the poorest and most miserable parish in the East End of London. Close-packed, crushed by the buttressed height of the railway viaduct, rendered airless by huge walls of factories, it at once banished lively '''interest''' from a stranger's mind and left only a dull oppression of the spirit.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21583239-real-science-lies-behind-fad-standing-up-work-standing-orders|title=Standing orders|passage=Over the past few years, however, '''interest''' has waxed again. A series of epidemiological studies, none big enough to be probative, but all pointing in the same direction, persuaded Emma Wilmot of the University of Leicester, in Britain, to carry out a meta-analysis. This is a technique that combines diverse studies in a statistically meaningful way.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180122122446/https://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21583239-real-science-lies-behind-fad-standing-up-work-standing-orders|archivedate=22 January 2018}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=Chico Harlan|volume=189|issue=2|page=30|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2013/jun/18/japan-solar-energy-fukushima-nuclear-renewable-abe|title=Japan pockets the subsidy{{nb...}}
|passage=Across Japan, technology companies and private investors are racing to install devices that until recently they had little '''interest''' in: solar panels. Massive solar parks are popping up as part of a rapid build-up that one developer likened to an "explosion."|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261026190359/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/jun/18/japan-solar-energy-fukushima-nuclear-renewable-abe|archivedate=26 October 2026}}
# {{senseid|en|benefit}} {{lb|en|often|in the plural}} [[advantage|Advantage]], [[welfare]], or [[something]] that [[serve]]s it.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|last=Kołakowski|first=Leszek|authorlink=Leszek Kołakowski|title=w:Main Currents of Marxism|volume=III: The Breakdown|origlang=pl|original=Główne nurty marksizmu: rozkład|translator=P[aul] S[teven] Falla|origyear=1976|publisher=w:Clarendon Press|location=w:Oxford|isbn=9780198245704|oclc=59183135|page=198|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/maincurrentsofma00kola/page/198|passage=The whole difference comes down to this, that Trotsky believed himself to 'represent the historical '''interests''' of the proletariat', while Stalin believed that he, Stalin, did so.}}
# {{senseid|en|any stake held by any type of stakeholder}} {{lb|en|countable}} An [[involvement]], [[claim]], [[right]], [[share]], [[stake]] in or [[link]] with a [[financial]], [[business]], or other [[undertaking]] or [[endeavor]].
#: {{hypo|en|self-interest|enlightened self-interest|public interest|;|vested interest|special interest|controlling interest|security interest}}
#: {{ux|en|When scientists and doctors write articles and when politicians run for office, they are required in many countries to declare any existing conflicts of '''interest''' (competing '''interests''').}}
#: {{ux|en|I have business '''interests''' in South Africa.}}
#: {{ux|en|She has an '''interest''' in the proceedings, and all stakeholders' '''interests''' must be protected.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2025 June 25|author=Christian Wolmar|title=How slow boats gradually gave way to rail|journal=RAIL|issue=1038|page=57|text=However he lost out, as other business '''interests''' whom he had alienated by his efforts to squeeze every penny of profit from the canal, supported the construction of the railway.}}
# {{senseid|en|something or someone as focus of attention}} {{lb|en|countable}} Something which, or someone whom, one is interested in.
#: {{ux|en|Lexicography is one of my '''interests'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Victorian furniture is an '''interest''' of mine.}}
#: {{ux|en|The main character's romantic '''interest''' will be played by a non-professional actor.}}
# {{senseid|en|worthiness of attention}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} Condition or quality of exciting concern or being of importance.
#* {{RQ:Coleridge Friend|date=7 September 1809|issue=4|page=52|passage=The Conscience indeed is already violated when to moral good or evil we oppose things possessing no moral '''interest''': {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|damages as compensation}} {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} [[injury|Injury]], or compensation for injury; [[damage]]s.
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=12|book=II|passage=How can this infinite beauty, power and goodnes admit any correspondencie or similitude with a thing so base and abject as we are, without extreme '''interest''' and manifest derogation from his divine greatnesse?}}
# {{senseid|en|persons and companies interested in any particular business or measure}} {{lb|en|often|in the plural}} The persons and companies interested in any particular business or measure, taken collectively.
#: {{cot|en|industry|trust|syndicate}}
#: {{ux|en|the iron '''interest''';&emsp; the cotton '''interest'''}}
#* {{quote-book |en |year=2023 |last=Brandon |first=David |title=The General Strike 1926: A New History |publisher=Pen and Sword Books Ltd |page=29 |isbn=9781399083980 |passage=The ‘Railway '''Interest'''’ in Parliament constituted a very powerful lobby at this time [mid to late nineteenth century]. It was pledged to put the railway case forward in all matters involving that industry that came before Parliament. This body of MPs was composed of men who were nearly all railway directors or major shareholders. Their influence waned in the early twentieth century but earlier they formed a phalanx of robust opposition to just about every proposal for government regulation of matters pertaining to railways.}}
# {{senseid|en|genre of factual short films}} {{lb|en|historical|usually|attributive}} A [[genre]] of factual [[short film]]s, generally more amusing than informative, especially those not covered by a more specific genre label.
#* '''1921''' Davidson Boughey, ''The Film Industry'' (London : Sir Isaac Pitman) [https://archive.org/details/filmindustry00bougrich/page/76/ p. 76]
#*: By '''interest''' films is meant a variety of subjects which cannot be classified under such recognized headings as fiction, travel, or topical. They include wonderful inventions, little known industries, applied art, feats of engineering, and other events capable of effective illustration.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 5 1924|author=[[w:Kevin O'Higgins|Kevin O'Higgins]]|titleurl=https://www.oireachtas.ie/en/debates/debate/dail/1924-03-05/23/|title=CENSORSHIP OF FILMS ACT, 1923|journal=Dáil debates|volume=6|number=22
|passage=The arrangements made ensured that the total cost of censorship could be kept down to one-fifth of a penny per foot of film censored (and even one-tenth of a penny per foot in cases of Topical, Travel, '''Interest''' and Educational Films).|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240127195506/https://www.oireachtas.ie/en/debates/debate/dail/1924-03-05/23/|archivedate=27 January 2024}}
#* '''1939''' March-April, J. Neill-Brown, [https://archive.org/details/cinetech04asso/page/n233/ "The Industry's Front Page"] ''The Cine-Technician'' (London) Vol. 4 no. 20 p. 200
#*: At  the  top  of  Charing  Cross Road  is  the  "Tatler,"  which  has specialised  for  a  long time  in  a  general  sort  of  program, built  up  of  about  15 minutes  of  news,  a  cartoon  (sometimes two),  an  '''interest''' picture,  occasionally  a  comedy,  and nearly  always  a documentary.

